# Suave

**Suave** is a smooth, readable programming language for scripts, data and web apps. It runs in the terminal on [Deno](https://deno.com) and in the browser, and it comes with a big standard library: **59 modules and about 1,050 functions**, including parsers and writers for **JSON, YAML, TOML, CSV, XML, INI and SUVL**, Suave's own data format. It also has **complex and imaginary numbers**, **quantum computing and quantum mechanics**, **pools** (values that are decided when they are used), **async/await**, and a built-in **package manager**.

```suave
# Suave looks like Python: colons, indentation, def, elif, lambda, class
use std.json
use std.table

class Dog:
    name: str
    age: num = 1

    def speak(self):
        return f"{self.name} is {self.age} and says woof!"

def describe(n):
    if n % 15 == 0:
        return "FizzBuzz"
    elif n % 3 == 0:
        return "Fizz"
    else:
        return str(n)

print(Dog("Rex").speak())
print([describe(n) for n in range(1, 8)])

people = json.parse('[{"name": "Ada", "age": 36}, {"name": "Bob", "age": 25}]')
table.from(people).sort_by("age", desc=True).print()
```

Suave also has **Lake** (a task runner: a `Lakefile` with tasks, like Make or Rake) and **Laker** (desktop apps from a Suave web app, like Tauri), see [docs/LAKE.md](#/suave-lake) and [docs/LAKER.md](#/suave-laker).

Examples to read: `examples/pdf-annotator/` (a PDF annotator) and `examples/keydown-editor/` (a Keydown editor), both one Suave file plus a plain `index.html`.

Suave files come in three kinds: **`.sv`** (programs and modules), **`.xsv`** (web components: props, script, template and style in one file) and **`.wsv`** (a web page or app), so Suave is also a small web framework. See [docs/WEB.md](#/suave-web).

It also has Rust-style enums, `match`, `Result` with `?`, traits and `{ braces }` for blocks, and you can mix the two styles.


## Why Suave

Suave aims at the everyday jobs people reach for Python, R, Julia, Clojure or Rust for, in one small language:

| You might use | for | In Suave |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Python | scripts, glue, quick tools | `suave run`, a REPL, `std.fs`, `std.process`, `std.cli`, readable code |
| R / pandas | tables and statistics | `std.table`, `std.stats`, `std.plot` (terminal charts and SVG) |
| Julia / NumPy | numbers and matrices | `std.math`, `std.linalg`, `std.bigint`, `std.rational`, `std.complex` |
| Clojure | data pipelines, lazy sequences | `\|>` pipes, `std.iter`, `std.func` (compose, curry, memoize, trampoline) |
| Rust | enums, pattern matching, `Result` and `?`, traits | all of those, plus type annotations that are checked as the program runs |
| Qiskit / QuTiP | quantum circuits and quantum mechanics | `std.quantum` (a state-vector simulator with Grover, Shor, teleportation) and `std.qm` (Schrodinger solver, hydrogen, spin) |
| npm / pip / cargo | packages | `suave add`, `suave install`, a lock file with checksums, registries that are just folders |
| JavaScript | web apps | runs in the browser; `std.dom` has a small state / view / update framework |

It is a young project (version 0.1), written in JavaScript so it runs anywhere Deno or a browser does. See [Where Suave is today](#where-suave-is-today) for the honest limits.

## Install (Mac, Linux)

The quickest way: paste this one line into Terminal. It downloads Suave, checks the download, installs [Deno](https://deno.com) if you need it, and sets everything up:

```sh
curl -fsSL https://radii-office.com/suave/install.sh | sh
```

Run the same line again, or type `suave upgrade`, to get the newest version. To remove it: `~/.suave/install.sh --uninstall`.

Already have this folder? Open Terminal in it and run:

```sh
./install.sh
```

(Or double-click **Install Suave.command** in Finder.) It installs [Deno](https://deno.com) if you do not have it, puts the commands `suave`, `lake` and `laker` in `~/.local/bin`, and makes sure new terminal windows can find them. Open a new terminal window afterwards and try `suave eval "1 + 2"`. If macOS says a file "cannot be opened because it is from an unidentified developer", the installer already clears that for this folder; if it still stops you, right-click the file and choose Open once. To remove everything again: `./install.sh --uninstall`.

Without installing: `deno run -A bin/suave.js run hello.sv`, or `deno task suave run hello.sv`. Windows: install Deno, then use `deno task install`.

## The command line

```
suave run <file.sv> [args]     run a program        (suave file.sv works too)
suave repl                     an interactive prompt (plain "suave" starts it)
suave eval "<code>"            run a bit of code
suave test [paths]             run test blocks in *_test.sv files
suave check <files>            syntax check
suave add <package>            install a package (suave init, install, remove, list, update, pack, publish)
suave new <name> [--web]       start a project
suave serve [dir] [port]       serve a folder
suave build <file.sv> -o dist  package a web app (index.html + suave.js)
suave modules                  list the standard library
suave doc std.json             what a module offers
suave convert data.yaml json   change a data file's format
```

Errors show the exact place, the source line and the call chain:

```
IndexError: Index 10 is out of range (size 3)
  --> app.sv:2
      2 | print(xs[10])
```

## A quick tour

```suave
// values
let n = 42                      // let: cannot change     var: can change
var total = 0
let text = "n is {n * 2}"       // "..." interpolates {expressions}; '...' does not
let nums = [1, 2, 3]; let person = {name: "Ada", "any key": 1}; let ids = #{1, 2}
let pair = (1, "two")           // tuples

// functions: defaults, named arguments, lambdas
fn greet(name, greeting = "Hello") = "{greeting}, {name}!"
print(greet("Ada"), greet(greeting: "Hi", name: "Bob"))
let double = |x| x * 2
print([1, 2, 3] |> map(double) |> sum())

// loops, ranges, comprehensions
for i in 1..=3 { total += i }
print([x * x for x in 1..6 if x % 2 == 1])

// errors are values (Result) and can be thrown
fn parse_age(s) {
    let n = s.to_int()
    if n == none { return Err("not a number: {s}") }
    Ok(n)
}
fn describe(s) { let age = parse_age(s)?; Ok("age {age}") }     // ? passes an Err upward
print(describe("36"), describe("x"))

// structs, enums, traits
struct Point { x, y = 0 }
impl Point { fn len(self) = (self.x ** 2 + self.y ** 2) ** 0.5 }
print(Point(3, 4).len())

// tests live next to the code
test "double works" { assert_eq(double(4), 8) }
```

More: [docs/LANGUAGE.md](#/suave-language) (the whole language), [docs/STDLIB.md](#/suave-stdlib) (every module), [docs/SUVL.md](#/suave-suvl) (the data format), [docs/WEB.md](#/suave-web) (web apps), `examples/`.

## Data formats

All the parsers are written for Suave (no outside libraries) and give errors with line and column. Every format round-trips through Suave values:

```suave
use std.data
print(data.convert("name: Ada\nlangs: [en, fr]", "yaml", "toml"))
data.save("config.suvl", {server: {host: "localhost", timeout: 90}})     // format from the file name
let cfg = data.load("config.toml")
```

| Module | Reads and writes |
| --- | --- |
| `std.json` | JSON (plus lenient mode, JSON Lines, big integers) |
| `std.yaml` | YAML 1.2: anchors, merge keys, block text, many documents |
| `std.toml` | TOML 1.0: tables, arrays of tables, dates |
| `std.suvl` | **SUVL**, Suave's own format (durations like `90s`, sizes like `64mb`, dates, blocks) |
| `std.csv` | CSV and TSV |
| `std.xml`, `std.ini` | XML trees and INI files |

## Web apps

```suave
use std.dom

fn view(count, dispatch) {
    dom.h("div", {}, [
        dom.h("h1", {}, ["Clicked {count} times"]),
        dom.h("button", {on_click: |e| dispatch("click")}, ["Click me"]),
    ])
}
fn update(count, msg) = if msg == "click" { count + 1 } else { count }

dom.app("#root", 0, view, update)
```

`suave build app.sv -o dist` writes `dist/index.html` and a single `suave.js` (about 280 KB, the whole language). Or put it in any page:

```html
<script src="suave.js"></script>
<script type="text/suave" src="app.sv"></script>
```

Open `playground/index.html` through a web server to try examples live. New to websites? `use std.web` has plain one-word helpers (`web.serve(...)`, `await web.get(url)`, `web.page(...)`, `web.say`, `web.button`), see docs/WEB.md! There is also a server side: `std.http` has a router (`app.get("/hi/:name", |req| ...)`) and `suave serve`.

## New in 0.2: imaginary numbers, quantum, pools, async and packages

```suave
use std.quantum as q
use std.async

// complex numbers are part of the language
print((3 + 4i) * (1 - 2i), sqrt(-4))               // 11 - 2i  2i

// a quantum computer
let bell = q.register(2).h(0).cx(0, 1)
print(bell, bell.sample(100))                       // 0.7071|00> + 0.7071|11>  {"00": 52, "11": 48}
print(q.shor(21).factors)                           // [3, 7]

// a pool is decided when it is first used
let weather = pool ["rain", "sun"]
let plans = [weather, weather]                      // still undecided
print("It will be {weather}")                       // decided now, and it stays

// async and await
async fn slow(x) { await async.delay(0.1); x * 2 }
print(await async.all([slow(1), slow(2), slow(3)]))   // [2, 4, 6] in 0.1 s, not 0.3
```

```sh
suave add mathx             # install a package (and what it needs), with a lock file
suave publish --to ./reg    # publish your own to a registry folder
```

Guides: [docs/QUANTUM.md](#/suave-quantum), [docs/POOLS.md](#/suave-pools), [docs/PM.md](#/suave-pm).

## Where Suave is today

Honest notes for version 0.1:

* **It is an interpreter** written in JavaScript. On this Mac, `fib(30)` takes about 0.26 s (CPython 3.13: about 0.1 s) and a 3-million-step loop about 0.25 s (CPython: 0.3 s). Fine for scripts, data work, small servers and web apps; not for heavy number crunching.
* **async/await** is built in, and the older callback style still works (`http.get(url, |res| ...)`, `time.after(1, f)`). Tasks take turns on one thread (like JavaScript); there are no threads.
* **Quantum** is a simulator on a normal computer: up to 22 qubits, no noise.
* **Types are checked while the program runs**, not before. Annotations like `fn f(x: int) -> str` are enforced at the call.
* **Packages:** the package manager works with folders, tarballs, GitHub and registry folders, but there is no official public registry yet.
* **`//` starts a comment**, so whole-number division is `div(7, 2)` (or `7.div(2)`).
* Deep recursion works to about 90,000 calls with the `bin/suave` launcher (about 8,000 with plain `deno run`).
* Tested on macOS with Deno 2.9 and Chrome. Firefox, Safari and Windows have not been tried yet.

## Tests

```sh
suave test                     # 70 tests: the language, the formats, the standard library, complex numbers, pools, async, quantum and the package manager
```

